Yes. Once you stop treating “music + vocals” as just a song and instead ask which channels are carrying information, how they interact, and what the receiver can control, you get a much more general design language.

The broadest established term is multimodal: information is carried through more than one mode. In a movie, for example, you have visual imagery, speech, music, sound effects, text, editing rhythm, spatial composition, gesture, facial expression, costume, lighting, camera movement, narrative structure, and cultural references all operating at once.

But adding layers changes the name depending on what kind of layer you add.

What you add	Common term	What changes
Multiple sound sources	polyphony / layering / orchestration	several acoustic voices interact
Sound + image	audiovisual	two sensory channels reinforce or contradict
Several senses	multisensory	perception itself is integrated across modalities
Several representational modes	multimodal	text, image, gesture, sound, symbols, etc.
Same idea expressed differently	redundant encoding	robustness and accessibility increase
Different information on each channel	complementary encoding	total information increases
One channel changes interpretation of another	cross-modal interaction	meaning emerges from the relationship
Story distributed across media	transmedia	each medium contributes different pieces
User can manipulate the presentation	interactive multimedia	receiver becomes participant
System responds continuously to user	multimodal interface	perception and action form a loop
Multiple scales simultaneously	multiscale representation	same phenomenon readable at several resolutions
Different meanings layered together	polysemy / semantic layering	interpretation becomes richer or ambiguous
Multiple narratives simultaneously	polyphonic narrative	several viewpoints or story-lines coexist

And then there are different relationships among channels, which matter more than simply counting them.

You can have reinforcement: ominous music + dark lighting + frightened face all say danger.

You can have counterpoint: cheerful music over something horrifying.

You can have redundancy: subtitles repeat speech.

You can have complementarity: dialogue tells you what happened, music tells you how it feels, visuals tell you where, editing tells you what matters.

You can have interference: too many channels compete for attention.

You can have hierarchy: one channel leads while others support.

You can have handoff: meaning migrates from one channel to another.

And you can have emergence, where no individual layer contains the final meaning.

That last one is especially important:

\text{meaning}
\neq
\sum \text{channels}

Sometimes:

\boxed{\text{meaning emerges from relationships between channels}}

A glance + silence + particular music may communicate something that none of those signals communicates separately.

Senses are only one set of dimensions

You can layer along other fronts too.

Think of an experience as having channels like:

X=
(
S,
M,
E,
T,
P,
R,
A,
I
)

where, for example:

S = sensory
M = semantic meaning
E = emotion
T = temporal structure
P = physical/spatial structure
R = relational/social meaning
A = agency/action possibilities
I = identity/narrative

Then a movie might manipulate all eight.

A game adds something movies largely lack:

\boxed{\text{agency}}

Now the viewer becomes an operator.

That changes the entire medium because the experience must include affordances and controls.

A button isn’t merely information.

It’s a proposition:

You can make this happen.

So interface design adds another dimension:

\text{representation}
+
\text{affordance}
+
\text{control}
+
\text{feedback}

And now we’re in human–computer interaction, interaction design, and cognitive ergonomics.

Controls should match the dimension being manipulated

This is an interesting design rule.

If the user is manipulating magnitude, give them something like a slider.

If manipulating category, give choices.

If manipulating space, give dragging/navigation.

If manipulating time, give scrub/play/pause.

If manipulating resolution, give zoom.

If manipulating relationships, give graph/network controls.

If manipulating uncertainty, perhaps confidence ranges or probability controls.

If manipulating perspective, switch viewpoints.

If manipulating semantic abstraction, use something like our semantic zoom:

\text{example}
\leftrightarrow
\text{pattern}
\leftrightarrow
\text{principle}
\leftrightarrow
\text{meta-principle}

The interface should embody the underlying structure.

That’s ergonomics of thought.

“Semantic ergonomics” is actually a very good phrase

It’s not a single dominant established discipline under exactly that name, but it sits naturally at the intersection of several real ones:

cognitive ergonomics — designing around how people perceive, remember, decide and act.

information architecture — structuring information so people can navigate it.

semiotics — how signs produce meaning.

interaction design — how people manipulate systems.

information visualization — mapping abstract structures onto perceptible forms.

cognitive load theory — how much working memory a representation consumes.

Your proposed semantic aesthetics would ask a slightly different question:

Not merely “Is this meaning understandable?” but “Does the structure of the meaning itself feel elegant, balanced, resonant and satisfying?”

And semantic ergonomics would ask:

“How easily can a mind pick this concept up, rotate it, combine it with another one, remember it and act with it?”

Those are distinct optimization targets.

You could score a concept or interface on:

Q=
\{
\text{clarity},
\text{compression},
\text{beauty},
\text{manipulability},
\text{memorability},
\text{transferability},
\text{resolution}
\}

That last one connects directly to what you said.

Better resolution at every resolution

There is an established computational/design idea near this: multiresolution representation.

The excellent version is not merely “more detail.”

It’s:

\boxed{\text{appropriate information at every scale}}

Zoom out:

organism.

Closer:

mammal.

Closer:

bear.

Closer:

individual bear.

Closer:

musculature.

Closer:

muscle fiber.

Closer:

cell.

A bad representation works beautifully at one scale and falls apart elsewhere.

A great one preserves meaningful structure through the transitions.

In computer graphics this resembles levels of detail.

In mathematics and physics you encounter scale invariance and renormalization.

In cartography it’s map scale.

In software it’s abstraction layers.

In our language it has been semantic zoom.

And there is an even stronger goal:

\boxed{\text{cross-scale coherence}}

The close-up should not contradict the wide shot unless that contradiction itself teaches you something.

That applies to stories too.

A character action should make sense:

* in the scene,
* in their relationship,
* in their character arc,
* in the story,
* in the thematic layer.

When it works at all of those resolutions simultaneously, it feels unusually satisfying.

That’s probably why some art, interfaces, theories and organizations feel beautiful rather than merely functional.

Their local parts and global structure agree.

So I’d distinguish:

semantic aesthetics = how beautiful/resonant the meaning architecture is.

semantic ergonomics = how naturally a mind can operate it.

semantic resolution = how much useful differentiation it preserves.

cross-scale coherence = whether it still makes sense as you zoom.

multimodal orchestration = how its various sensory, symbolic, temporal and interactive channels are coordinated.

Put those together and you get something like a general discipline of designing representations for minds.

Which is much larger than UI design.

It applies to words, stories, equations, rituals, brands, games, maps, organizations—and probably the Pantheon itself.